---
alwaysApply: true
---

CRITICAL WORKFLOW
- Start in plan mode: write an implementation plan before coding.
- Use `venv_linux` for all Python command execution.
- Perform research before implementation.

CODE GENERATION LIMITS
- Files ≤ 500 lines; split earlier if approaching.
- Functions ≤ 50 lines; single responsibility.
- Classes ≤ 100 lines; one concept per class.
- Max line length 100 chars (ruff enforced).
- Use type hints on every function signature.
- Use absolute imports only (no relative imports).
- Use `uv` for package management; never edit `pyproject.toml` directly.

STYLE & NAMING
- snake_case for functions/variables, PascalCase for classes, UPPER_SNAKE_CASE for constants.
- Private members start with a leading underscore.
- Prefer simple, descriptive names and shallow logic.

DRY ENFORCEMENT
- Search the codebase with `rg` for similar logic before writing new code.
- Refactor or extract shared utilities instead of duplicating logic.
- Update existing call sites to use shared utilities when appropriate.

TEST-DRIVEN DEVELOPMENT
- Write test → see it fail → minimal code to pass → refactor → repeat.
- Test production code paths; avoid test-only mock implementations.
- Keep tests next to code; shared fixtures in `conftest.py`.
- Target ≥ 80% coverage with emphasis on critical paths.

ERROR HANDLING
- Use domain-specific exception classes.
- Validate early and fail fast with clear errors.
- Return structured error info from boundaries.

GIT & BRANCHING
- Branches: main (prod), feature/*, fix/*, docs/*, refactor/*.
- Commits must follow Conventional Commit format (see commits.mdc).

NON-NEGOTIABLES
- Never commit secrets.
- Use HTTPS for external calls.
- Parameterize all DB queries.
- Hash passwords with bcrypt.
- Generate tokens with `secrets`.

HOUSEKEEPING
- Make scripts executable.
- Remove nonessential comments and docstrings; keep only what explains “why”.
- Prefer small, cohesive modules grouped by feature.